            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Background: Three High-Frequency Pain Points in Agency Account Opening</h2>
<p>In serving overseas enterprises, Yiyu Shumei has identified three persistent issues in agency account opening services:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Information Scattered</strong>: Opening progress, client requirements, and qualification review status are distributed across different chat records, spreadsheets, and emails. Operations staff spend significant time daily just "finding information."</li>
<li><strong>Ambiguous Responsibilities</strong>: Each account opening involves sales, channel, finance, and technical teams. When problems arise, there's often a vacuum where everyone assumes someone else handled it.</li>
<li><strong>Delayed Exception Detection</strong>: Clients only discover a blockage when they proactively ask about progress—by then, service experience and follow-up cooperation have already suffered.</li>
</ul>
<p>These three issues fundamentally stem from <strong>opaque information and invisible processes</strong>. The solution is straightforward: move the entire account opening workflow onto a systematic Kanban board where everyone can see current status, what needs to happen next, and where problems might emerge.</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Steps</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Design the Kanban Framework with Defined Columns</h3>
<p>Columns represent different stages of the account opening process. Recommended six-column setup:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Column</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Pending Verification</strong></td>
<td>Client submitted application, qualification not yet verified</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Qualification Review</strong></td>
<td>Verifying business license, KYC materials, etc.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Channel Coordination</strong></td>
<td>Communicating with platforms (Meta/Google/TikTok) or Tier-1 agents</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Account Creation</strong></td>
<td>Platform-side account creation in progress</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Account Delivery</strong></td>
<td>Account opened and delivered to client</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Completed/Exception</strong></td>
<td>Normal closure or requiring special handling</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: Column names shouldn't be too abstract. Vague terms like "In Progress" or "Pending" lack informational value. Each column name should let team members instantly determine "where is this currently stuck."</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 2: Define Required Fields for Each Card</h3>
<p>Each card represents an account opening task and must include these fields when moved to the board:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Client Name</strong> (anonymized: Client A, Client B)</li>
<li><strong>Platform</strong> (Meta/Google/TikTok, etc.)</li>
<li><strong>Request Type</strong> (new account/add account/reopen account)</li>
<li><strong>Submission Time</strong></li>
<li><strong>Expected Completion Time</strong> (agreed with client)</li>
<li><strong>Responsible Person</strong> (assigned to specific individual)</li>
<li><strong>Current Blockage</strong> (or "None" if clear)</li>
</ul>
<p>These fields determine the automation level of card migration. Cards with incomplete fields render the Kanban ineffective.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 3: Establish Status Migration Rules</h3>
<p>Not all cards can freely migrate to the next column. Define clear migration conditions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pending Verification → Qualification Review</strong>: Client provides complete materials</li>
<li><strong>Qualification Review → Channel Coordination</strong>: Internal review passed</li>
<li><strong>Channel Coordination → Account Creation</strong>: Channel confirmed account can be opened</li>
<li><strong>Account Creation → Account Delivery</strong>: Platform returns account credentials</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operations Reminder</strong>: Set "overdue alerts" for each migration node. For example, if qualification review isn't approved within 2 business days, the card automatically turns red or triggers an alert. This is far more efficient than waiting for client inquiries.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 4: Establish Daily Kanban Maintenance Routine</h3>
<p>Building the Kanban is just the first step—daily maintenance is critical:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Daily Standup (5 minutes)</strong>: Review "Pending Verification" and "Exception" columns to ensure no tasks are missed</li>
<li><strong>Immediate Card Updates</strong>: Update card status within 5 minutes of completing each step</li>
<li><strong>Weekly Cleanup</strong>: Every Friday, check all cards for overdue migrations and document reasons</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h3>Step 5: Exception Handling Process</h3>
<p>When a card enters the "Exception" column, follow this process:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Document Exception Reason</strong>: Platform rejection, qualification mismatch, slow channel response, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Assign Follow-up Person</strong>: Don't leave exceptions floating—assign a single responsible party</li>
<li><strong>Set Resolution Timeline</strong>: Based on exception type, establish 24-48 hour resolution targets</li>
<li><strong>Escalation Mechanism</strong>: Auto-escalate to supervisor if unresolved beyond timeline</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<p><strong>Q: With many clients, won't the Kanban become too cluttered?</strong></p>
<p>A: It's recommended to set up multiple swimlanes by platform or client type, with independent Kanbans within each lane. For example, Lane 1 handles Meta accounts exclusively, Lane 2 handles Google accounts—keeping things organized.</p>
<p><strong>Q: I already have spreadsheets managing this. Do I still need a Kanban?</strong></p>
<p>A: Spreadsheets are better for recording "results" while Kanbans track "process." If your spreadsheets can reflect each stage's current status and responsible person in real-time, then your spreadsheet is already a Kanban. If not, consider migrating to Kanban tools (Feishu Multi-dimensional Tables, Trello, etc.).</p>
<p><strong>Q: Team members won't cooperate with updates. What do I do?</strong></p>
<p>A: The Kanban relies on team consensus. Consider piloting for 1-2 weeks first, collecting feedback to adjust rules, then formally implementing. Tie Kanban updates to workflow—e.g., cards not updated mean tasks aren't considered complete.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>A systematic Kanban isn't just another management tool—it's about <strong>making implicit processes explicit</strong>. Operations staff no longer need to scroll through chat history for progress updates, supervisors don't need to ask who's stuck where, and clients no longer need to inquire to learn about progress.</p>
<p>The core value of a Kanban can be summed up in one sentence: <strong>Let everyone know at any moment where things stand, who's responsible for next steps, and whether there are problems.</strong> Implementing this SOP should reduce client complaints caused by information asymmetry by at least 60%.</p>
